The purge path is traced end to end, but the REST handler for a plain `DELETE` (without `purge`) is not confirmed. I used up the search budget before locating the generic REST DELETE dispatch, so the chain below is for the purge path.

**Declarations on the path (best first):**

1. **Frontend request:** `deleteVisit(visitUuid)` sends `DELETE ${restBaseUrl}/visit/${visitUuid}` with no `purge` parameter. @file:{openmrs-esm-patient-chart::packages/esm-patient-chart-app/src/visit/visits-widget/visit.resource.tsx:49-53}
2. **Frontend caller:** the `useDeleteVisit` hook calls `deleteVisit(activeVisit?.uuid)` at line 59. @file:{openmrs-esm-patient-chart::packages/esm-patient-chart-app/src/visit/hooks/useDeleteVisit.tsx:59}
3. **Server REST handler:** `VisitResource1_9.purge(Visit, RequestContext)` returns early on null and otherwise calls `Context.getVisitService().purgeVisit(visit)`. @file:{openmrs-module-webservices.rest::omod/src/main/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/resource/openmrs1_9/VisitResource1_9.java:291-296}
4. **Core service implementation:** `VisitServiceImpl.purgeVisit(Visit)` returns if the visit is unsaved, throws `APIException("Visit.purge.inUse")` if the visit has encounters, and otherwise calls `dao.deleteVisit(visit)`. @file:{openmrs-core::api/src/main/java/org/openmrs/api/impl/VisitServiceImpl.java:219-231}
5. **Service interface:** `VisitService.purgeVisit(Visit)`. @file:{openmrs-core::api/src/main/java/org/openmrs/api/VisitService.java:284}
6. **Data-access interface:** `VisitDAO.deleteVisit(Visit)`. @file:{openmrs-core::api/src/main/java/org/openmrs/api/db/VisitDAO.java:92}
7. **Data-access implementation (the database write):** `HibernateVisitDAO.deleteVisit(Visit)` is `@Transactional` and calls `getCurrentSession().remove(visit)`. @file:{openmrs-core::api/src/main/java/org/openmrs/api/db/hibernate/HibernateVisitDAO.java:174-178}

**Caveats:**

- **Void vs. purge is unresolved.** The frontend sends no `purge` flag, so a plain `DELETE` may not reach `VisitResource1_9.purge` at all. It may instead go through the generic REST delete, which could call `VisitServiceImpl.voidVisit` (lines 206-209), which only calls `dao.saveVisit`. I did not confirm which applies.
- **Generic REST dispatch not found.** I did not locate the base-class DELETE controller in openmrs-module-webservices.rest.
- **Repository names.** Falkon required the `github.com/openmrs/` prefix on the `repo` parameter, which I used for the calls; the citations above use the short names as requested.

